<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Dual-pane (list + detail) pane budget — compact dual-pane band (673dp <= width < 900dp).

    The DEFAULT configuration deliberately carries the compact numbers: the dual-pane
    layout only exists behind the resource qualifier
    res/layout-w673dp-h480dp/activity_index.xml, so no configuration narrower than
    673dp can ever read these values. Windows >= 900dp read res/values-w900dp/dimens.xml
    instead: these five dimens have exactly two candidate directories in this module —
    this unqualified default and values-w900dp/ — and a qualified directory that matches
    the configuration always beats the unqualified default, while w900dp contradicts (and
    so is dropped for) any window below 900dp. Both halves are pinned behaviourally by
    app/src/test/java/com/difft/android/DualPaneBudgetResourceInvariantTest.kt.

    INVARIANT — the qualifier floor must cover the pane-budget feasibility floor:

        dual_pane_list_min_width + dual_pane_detail_min_width
        == 280 + 320 == 600dp
        <= the w673dp component of res/layout-w673dp-h480dp/

    673 dates from the 72dp navigation rail (+ 1dp seam) that used to sit beside the panes;
    the home tabs now float over the list pane, and whether the 600-672dp band should gain
    dual pane is a separate decision. Raising a minimum above the gate REQUIRES renaming
    that layout directory (and WindowSizeClassUtil.DUAL_PANE_MIN_WIDTH_DP). Pinned by
    app/src/test/java/com/difft/android/DualPaneBudgetResourceInvariantTest.kt.

    320dp is the declared static floor only: it can occur solely on 673-712dp windows
    (in practice multi-window and the narrowest unfolded foldables), where the divider's
    in-range drag is inert. Interactive resizing is clamped separately by
    dual_pane_detail_drag_min_width below.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- Seam between the list and detail panes (a floating overlay, takes no layout width). -->
    <dimen name="dual_pane_divider_width">1dp</dimen>

    <!-- Initial list-pane width; IndexActivity.applyListPaneWidth() re-applies it at
         runtime (user-drag ratio and large-font 50/50 override it, clamped to the
         min widths below). -->
    <dimen name="dual_pane_list_default_width">280dp</dimen>

    <!-- Floor terms of the invariant above, enforced at runtime by
         IndexActivity.applyListPaneWidth() / the drag clamp. The DETAIL minimum wins
         when both cannot be met: the detail pane holds fixed-width message content
         that clips, while the list rows compress gracefully. -->
    <dimen name="dual_pane_list_min_width">280dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="dual_pane_detail_min_width">320dp</dimen>

    <!-- Drag clamp floor for the detail pane, band-independent and intentionally above
         dual_pane_detail_min_width: incoming rows hosting the 270dp fixed-width bubbles
         (contact / audio / attachment) need 26 (start offset) + 270 + 40 (end margin)
         = 336dp to render unclipped, 340dp with the voice-speed button. Users must not
         be able to drag the detail pane into that clipping zone; only the static budget
         on 673-712dp windows (in-range drag inert there) may go below. -->
    <dimen name="dual_pane_detail_drag_min_width">360dp</dimen>
    <!-- Me page top gap where it shows no title row (phones). -->
    <dimen name="me_headerless_top_gap">25dp</dimen>

</resources>
